# Step 6: Prepare a folder for exports

1. In File Explorer, create the folder C:\SQLExport.

2.  If a later step says “Access is denied” or “cannot open file”, 
    right-click the folder, choose Properties, then Security, and give the SQL Server service account 
    (usually NT Service\MSSQLSERVER) Modify permission.

# Step 7: Export and import one table (data only)

    Use bcp, a command-line tool that installs with SQL Server. Open Command Prompt (not SSMS) and run

    bcp DemoDB.dbo.employees out C:\SQLExport\employees.csv -c -t, -T -S localhost

    Now import it into a copy of the table. In SSMS, create an empty copy first:

    USE DemoDB;
    GO
    SELECT * INTO dbo.employees_copy FROM dbo.employees WHERE 1 = 0;   -- same columns, no rows
    GO

    Then back in Command Prompt:

    bcp DemoDB.dbo.employees_copy in C:\SQLExport\employees.csv -c -t, -T -S localhost -E

    # Step 8: Export and import the whole database 
                
              Option A: Backup and restore (a .bak file, the physical copy)

    USE master;
GO
BACKUP DATABASE DemoDB TO DISK = 'C:\SQLExport\DemoDB.bak'
WITH COMPRESSION, CHECKSUM, INIT;
GO


